tel 1.0

base degustation
import soundness

info   a reader of compiled Scala: TASTy files as plain data, resolved against a classpath, and erased
detail
    _Degustation_ reads what the Scala compiler leaves behind. Every Scala 3 class file is
    accompanied by a **TASTy** file (`Option.tasty` beside `Option.class`), the compiler's
    typed syntax tree pickled into a compact binary form: every definition, its modifiers and
    annotations, its parameter and result types, the parents of every class, and the bodies
    of inline and macro definitions. It is the complete record of a module's API, and the
    thing any tool which must understand compiled Scala — an API walker, a documentation
    renderer, a compatibility checker — needs to read. Degustation reads it without running
    the compiler: the only dependency is `tasty-core`, the small library which knows the
    format's byte layout, so a reading runs on the JVM, in a browser and in a native binary.

    The reader yields **values**. `Unpickler(data)` turns the bytes of one file into an
    `Unpickler.File` of `Pickled.Tree`s, `Pickled.Type`s and `Pickled.Name`s, exactly as the
    format lays them out and with no symbol table behind them: a reference to a local
    definition is the address of its definition node, and a reference to anything else is a
    name against a prefix type, as the file spells it. A `Universe` answers the questions the
    bytes alone cannot — what a name refers to, which class declares a selected member, how a
    class linearizes — by loading further files lazily from a `Universe.Source`, a classpath
    reduced to the two operations of reading an entry and listing a directory. Over a
    universe, an `Erasure` computes Scala 3's type erasure, so that a member's erased
    signature — `(scala.Function1)scala.Option` — is spelled as the compiler would spell it.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val file: Unpickler.File = Unpickler(cp"/scala/Option.tasty".read[Data])
    val scope: Scope = degustation.Universe.index(t"scala/Option.tasty", file)

    scope.names.values.filter(_.s.startsWith("scala.Option."))
    ```

    On top of the reader sits the **atomizer**, the reason the library exists: `Degustation.
    atomize` applies the rules of LIRA's Scala discipline (`tasty/2`) to a module's files,
    cutting its API into `ScalaAtom`s — each a stable key such as `fixture.Overloads.f(scala.
    Int)scala.Int`, a canonical binary encoding whose hash is the atom's value, and, for an
    inline body, the `ScalaReference`s it makes — so that two versions of a library can be
    compared by set arithmetic. `Inspection.atomize` is the same over files and a classpath
    on disk.

    The library's parts, by what each platform can do:

    - `degustation.tasty` — the data model (`Pickled`), the reader (`Unpickler`), the
      classfile reader (`Classfile`), the classpath universe (`Universe.Source`, `Scope`,
      `Universe.ClassInfo`, `Member`, `Kind`, `Resolution`, `Universe`), erasure (`Erasure`), the
      atomizer (`Degustation`) and its output (`ScalaAtom`, `ScalaReference`). Compiles for
      the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native; nothing in it touches a filesystem.
    - `degustation.core` — `Inspection`, the filesystem face: `.tasty` files and classpath
      directories read through galilei's filesystem backend, jars through zeppelin. JVM and
      Scala Native. On the JVM the JDK's own classes are read from the running JVM's class
      loader; on Native there is no JVM to read them from, so a jar of the JDK's class files
      must be on the classpath.

    Everything but `Universe` and `Classfile` is exported to the `soundness` umbrella; those
    two keep their simple names for anthology and mandible there, and are reached as
    `degustation.Universe` and `degustation.Classfile`. The library has no choice packages and
    one given, `Inspection.Error.communicable`, which is in implicit scope.

refer degustation.Unpickler
refer degustation.Pickled
refer degustation.Universe
refer degustation.Erasure
refer degustation.Degustation
refer degustation.Inspection

entry .Unpickler   reads a `.tasty` file's bytes into the `Pickled` model: trees, definitions and binders by address
  detail
      `Unpickler(data)` checks the file's header, decodes its name table and unpickles its
      ASTs section into `Pickled.Tree`s; the positions, comments and attributes sections are
      skipped, since nothing in them is API. Every shared subtree (`SHAREDtype`, `SHAREDterm`)
      is resolved to the tree it shares, memoized by address, so the result is a plain tree in
      which only the identity-bearing nodes — definitions, binders, recursive types — keep
      their addresses. The grammar followed is the one `TastyFormat` documents; where the
      compiler's own unpickler departs from it to build symbols or re-type nodes, this reader
      keeps what the file says.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val file: Unpickler.File = Unpickler(cp"/scala/Option.tasty".read[Data])
      file.statements
      ```

      A file whose header is not a TASTy header, or whose version the embedded `tasty-core`
      cannot read, throws `dotty.tools.tasty.UnpickleException`; so does a node which does
      not read to its recorded end, which means the grammar has drifted from the file's and is
      reported rather than silently misread. The bytes are copied once into a JVM array
      before reading.
  refer degustation.Pickled
  refer degustation.Scope
  entry .apply   unpickles one file's bytes into its `File`
      Reads the header, the names and the ASTs of `data`. A file with no ASTs section (which
      a valid file never lacks) gives an empty `File`.
  entry .File   the ASTs of one file: top-level statements, every definition by address, every binder by address
    detail
        A `File` is what `Unpickler(data)` returns and what `Universe.index` indexes into a
        `Scope`. `#statements` are the file's top-level trees, each a `Pickled.Tree.Package`;
        `#definitions` maps the address of every definition node to the node, so a local
        reference (`Type.TermRefDirect` and its kin) can be followed to the definition it
        names; `#binders` maps the address of every methodic, lambda or recursive type to it,
        for `Type.Param` and `Type.RecursiveThis` references to resolve against.
    entry #statements   the top-level trees, in file order
    entry #definitions   every definition node — `TypeDef`, `ValDef`, `DefDef`, `TypeParam`, `Param`, `Bind` — by its address
    entry #binders   every `Type.Method`, `Type.Poly`, `Type.Lambda` and `Type.Recursive`, by its address

entry .Pickled   the TASTy data model: names, types, trees and modifiers as the format lays them out
  detail
      `Pickled` holds the types which model a `.tasty` file as data, with no symbol table
      behind them: a `Name` from the name table, a `Type`, a `Tree`, the `Modifiers` of a
      definition, and the smaller enumerations they are built from. A reference to a local
      definition is the address of its definition node; a reference to anything else is a
      name and a prefix type, as the file spells it. Everything a semantic question needs —
      what a name refers to, what a type erases to, which class declares a selected member —
      is answered elsewhere, by `Universe` and `Erasure`.

      A few forms are not pickled at all but made by the reader or the universe for their own
      use, and say so: `Type.AppliedTerms`, `Type.Opaque`, `Type.Abstraction`, `Type.Foreign`,
      `Flag.ParamAccessor` and `Tree.Path`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val tpe: Pickled.Type =
        Pickled.Type.Applied(
          Pickled.Type.TypeRef(t"Option", Pickled.Type.TermRefPkg(t"scala")),
          List(Pickled.Type.TypeRef(t"Int", Pickled.Type.TermRefPkg(t"scala"))))

      degustation.Universe.className(tpe)   // t"scala.Option"
      ```
  refer degustation.Unpickler
  refer degustation.Universe
  entry .Name   a name from the name table, structured as the compiler's name kinds structure it
    detail
        A `Name` is `Simple(value)` for a plain name, or one of the compiler's derived kinds
        over another name: `Qualified(prefix, selector)` for `a.b`, `Expanded` and
        `ExpandPrefix` for the `$$`- and `$`-separated expansions of a private or local name,
        `Unique(separator, number, original)` for a fresh name, `DefaultGetter(method, index)`
        for a default argument's getter, `SuperAccessor`, `InlineAccessor`, `ObjectClass` (the
        `X$` of a module class) and `BodyRetainer` wrappers, and `Signed(original, target,
        result, params)` — a term name carrying the erased signature of the member it refers
        to, which is how a reference to an overloaded method says which alternative it means.

        `#text` renders a name as the compiler's `Name#toString` does, so what a stack frame or
        a classfile shows can be matched against it; `#spelled` is the `fullName` spelling,
        which differs only for a constructor's default getter; `#unsigned` drops a signature,
        and `#signature` is the signature a signed name carries, spelled `(paramSigs)resultSig`
        exactly as the keys of `ScalaAtom`s spell theirs.
    entry #text   the name as the compiler's `Name#toString` renders it
        `Simple` is its value; `Qualified` joins with `.`; `Expanded` with `$$` and
        `ExpandPrefix` with `$`; a `Unique` name is `original`, separator, number (or `$3$`
        for a fresh name with nothing to derive from); a `DefaultGetter` is `method$default$N`
        with a constructor's `<init>` mangled to `$lessinit$greater`; `SuperAccessor`,
        `InlineAccessor` and `BodyRetainer` put `super$`, `inline$` and `$retainedBody` on
        the last segment of a qualified name; `ObjectClass` appends `$`; and a `Signed` name
        renders as its original.
    entry #spelled   the name as the compiler's `fullName` spells it
        The same as `#text`, except that a constructor's default getter keeps `<init>`
        unmangled: `<init>$default$1`. This is the spelling `Scope#names` and `ScalaAtom`
        keys use.
    entry #unsigned   the name without its signature, if it had one
    entry #signature   the erased signature a signed name carries, or `Unset`
        Spelled `(paramSigs)resultSig`, each parameter signature a fully-qualified erased
        class name or a type-parameter count — the spelling `Erasure` produces for a
        declaration, so the two sides of a reference agree by construction.
  entry .ParamSig   one component of a signed name's erased signature
    detail
        `Erased(name)` is a fully-qualified erased type name; `TypeParams(count)` is the
        length of a type-parameter section. `#text` renders either as the signature spells it:
        the name, or the count as digits.
    entry #text   the component as it appears in a spelled signature
  entry .Constant   a literal constant: unit, booleans, null, the numeric and character primitives, a string or a class
    detail
        The cases are `UnitValue`, `FalseValue`, `TrueValue`, `NullValue`, `ByteValue`,
        `ShortValue`, `CharValue`, `IntValue`, `LongValue`, `FloatValue`, `DoubleValue`,
        `StringValue(value: Text)` and `ClassValue(tpe: Type)`, which is what a `classOf[T]`
        literal pickles as.
    entry #className   the fully-qualified class the constant's type is a reference to
        `scala.Unit`, `scala.Boolean`, `scala.Null`, `scala.Int` and the other primitives,
        `java.lang.String` for a string and `java.lang.Class` for a class literal.
  entry .Variance   `Invariant`, `Covariant` or `Contravariant`, as a type lambda's parameters carry them
  entry .Clause   the kind of a method type's parameter clause: `Plain`, `Implicit` or `Contextual`
  entry .Type   a type as pickled: references by address or by name against a prefix, and the type constructors
    detail
        References come in three families. A **local** reference names a definition in the
        same file by the address of its node: `TermRefDirect(address)` and
        `TypeRefDirect(address)`, or `TermRefLocal(address, prefix)` and `TypeRefLocal(address,
        prefix)` when a prefix is recorded too. A **package member** is named outright:
        `TermRefPkg(name)` and `TypeRefPkg(name)` carry a fully-qualified name. Anything else
        is a name against a prefix type: `TermRef(name, prefix)` with a `Name` that may be
        signed, `TypeRef(name, prefix)`, and the `TermRefIn` and `TypeRefIn` forms which also
        record the class the member was selected in.

        The constructors are the format's: `This(cls)` and `Super(thisType, underlying)`;
        `Recursive(address, parent)` and `RecursiveThis(address)` for a recursive refinement;
        `Refined(parent, name, info)`; `Applied(constructor, arguments)`; `Bounds(low, high,
        variances)`, where an absent `high` means an alias of `low`; `Annotated(underlying,
        annotation)`; `And` and `Or`; `Match(bound, scrutinee, cases)` and `MatchCase(pattern,
        rhs)`; `Flexible(underlying)` for a Java null-flexible type; `ByName(underlying)`;
        `Literal(constant)`; and `Bind(address, name, bounds)`. The binders — `Method(address,
        names, params, result, clause)`, `Poly(address, names, bounds, result)` and
        `Lambda(address, names, bounds, result)` — carry the address a `Param(binder, number)`
        inside them refers back to, and `Unpickler.File#binders` finds them by it.

        Four forms are not pickled but made by the reader or the universe: `AppliedTerms
        (function, arguments)`, the type of a term application whose function's type is not
        yet resolved; `Opaque(alias, bounds)`, an opaque type's right-hand side with the bounds
        it is seen through from outside; `Abstraction(params, names, bounds, result)`, a type
        lambda as a lambda type *tree* spells it, binding its parameters as definitions by
        address; and `Foreign(path, tpe)`, a type pinned to the file it was read from, so its
        local references still resolve when it is carried into another file's types — a type
        argument substituted into an alias, say.

        `Universe.resolve` says what a type refers to; `Universe.transform` and
        `Universe.mapChildren` rewrite one; `Erasure#erase` erases one.
  entry .Flag   a modifier flag, named as the format's tags are
    detail
        `Private`, `Protected`, `Abstract`, `Final`, `Sealed`, `Case`, `Implicit`, `Given`,
        `Erased`, `Lazy`, `Override`, `Opaque`, `Inline`, `Macro`, `InlineProxy`, `Static`,
        `Object`, `Trait`, `Enum`, `Local`, `Synthetic`, `Artifact`, `Mutable`,
        `FieldAccessor`, `CaseAccessor`, `Covariant`, `Contravariant`, `HasDefault`, `Stable`,
        `Extension`, `ParamSetter`, `ParamAlias`, `Exported`, `Open`, `Invisible`,
        `Transparent`, `Infix`, `Tracked`, `Into` and `AbsOverride` are the format's own.
        `ParamAccessor` is not a TASTy modifier: the universe adds it to mark a class
        parameter among its class's members, as the compiler's flag of that name does.
  entry .Annotation   an annotation on a definition: its type and the tree which constructs it
    entry #tpe   the annotation's class type
    entry #tree   the constructor call, as pickled
  entry .Modifiers   a definition's flags, its `private[X]` qualifier and its annotations
    detail
        Every `Tree.TypeDef`, `ValDef`, `DefDef`, `TypeParam`, `Param` and `Bind` carries
        one. `#has(flag)` tests a flag; `#privateWithin` is the type a `private[X]` or
        `protected[X]` qualifier names, or `Unset`; `.empty` has no flags, no qualifier and no
        annotations.
    entry .empty   no flags, no qualifier, no annotations
    entry #flags   the definition's flags, as a set
    entry #privateWithin   the `X` of `private[X]` or `protected[X]`, as a type, or `Unset`
    entry #annotations   the definition's annotations, in order
    entry #has   whether a flag is set
  entry .Selector   one selector of an `import` or `export`: `Imported(name)`, `Renamed(to)` or `Bounded(tpe)`
  entry .Tree   a tree as pickled: definitions, templates, terms, patterns and type trees
    detail
        A file's top-level trees are `Package(path, statements)`. Definitions are
        `TypeDef(address, name, rhs, modifiers)` — a class when `rhs` is a `Template
        (typeParams, params, parents, self, body)`, else a type alias, bounds or opaque type
        — `ValDef(address, name, tpt, rhs, modifiers)`, `DefDef(address, name, clauses, returns,
        rhs, modifiers)` with its clauses as lists of `TypeParam` or `Param` trees, and
        `Import` and `Export` with their `Selector`s. Each definition carries the address its
        local references name it by.

        Terms are the usual Scala forms — `Ident`, `Select`, `SelectIn`, `QualifiedThis`,
        `New`, `Apply`, `TypeApply`, `ApplySigPoly`, `Typed`, `Assign`, `Block`, `Inlined`,
        `Lambda`, `If`, `Match`, `Try`, `Return`, `While`, `Repeated`, `Throw`, `NamedArg`,
        `SuperRef`, `SelectOuter`, `Elided` — with the quotation forms `Quote`, `Splice`,
        `SplicePattern`, `QuotePattern` and `Hole`, and the pattern forms `Bind`,
        `Alternative`, `Unapply` and `CaseDef`. `Match` carries a `Tree.MatchKind`.

        Type trees are `IdentTpt`, `SelectTpt`, `SingletonTpt`, `RefinedTpt`, `AppliedTpt`,
        `LambdaTpt`, `BoundsTpt`, `AnnotatedTpt`, `MatchTpt`, `ByNameTpt` and `ExplicitTpt`;
        `#isTypeTree` is whether a tree is one of them. `Path(tpe)` is not a pickled form: a
        path or constant in tree position, whose whole content is a type.

        `Universe.typeOf` is the type a type tree denotes, `Universe.termType` the type of a
        term used as a prefix, and `Universe.children` a tree's immediate subtrees.
    entry .MatchKind   how a `Match` was written: `Plain`, `Inline`, `Implicit` or `Sub`
    entry #isTypeTree   whether this is a type tree, one the compiler would read with `readTpt`

entry .Scope   a loaded `.tasty` file, indexed: every definition's full name, locality and owner by address
  detail
      `Universe.index(path, file)` makes a `Scope` from an `Unpickler.File`, and
      `Universe#scope(path)` loads and indexes one from a `Universe.Source`. It holds the `#path`
      the file was read from, the `#file` itself, `#names` — the fully-qualified name of every
      definition a reference from outside a term body can reach, by address, spelled as the
      compiler's `fullName` spells it (a method's parameters as `pkg.Class._$param`) — the
      `#locals`, the definitions inside term bodies, and `#owners`, each definition's owner:
      the address of the definition enclosing it, or the name of its package at the top.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val scope: Scope =
        degustation.Universe.index(t"scala/Option.tasty", Unpickler(cp"/scala/Option.tasty".read[Data]))

      scope.names.values.filter(_.s.startsWith("scala.Option."))
      ```

      `Universe#resolve` takes a scope as the context in which a type's local references are
      followed; a type carried into another file keeps its own scope as a `Type.Foreign`.
  refer degustation.Universe
  refer degustation.Unpickler
  entry #path   the classpath-relative path the file was read from
  entry #file   the unpickled file
  entry #names   the fully-qualified name of every reachable definition, by address
  entry #locals   the addresses of the definitions inside term bodies
  entry #owners   each definition's owner: an enclosing definition's address, or a package name at the top
  entry #definition   the definition node at an address, or `Unset`
  entry #binder   the methodic, lambda or recursive type at an address, or `Unset`
  entry #within   whether the definition at one address is enclosed, at any depth, by the one at another
  entry #enclosingClass   the address of the nearest enclosing class of a definition, or `Unset` at the top

entry .Kind   the kind of a class member: `Val`, `Def`, `Type` or `Class`
    `Universe#memberIn` and `Universe#packageObjectMember` take a set of kinds to search for,
    so a type member and a term member of the same name are told apart.

entry .Member   one member of a class, by declaration: its name, kind, declared type, flags and address
  detail
      A `Universe.ClassInfo#members` entry. `#tpe` is the member's declared type — for a method, the
      methodic type its clauses spell, built by `Universe.methodType` — and `#address` finds
      the declaring tree in the class's `Scope`. For a member read from a Java classfile,
      `#erased` is the erased signature its descriptor already is, and `#address` is `-1`;
      `Erasure#signature(owner, member)` uses whichever is there.
  entry #name   the member's name, as `Name#spelled` spells it
  entry #kind   `Val`, `Def`, `Type` or `Class`
  entry #tpe   the declared type: a methodic type for a method, the alias or bounds for a type member
  entry #flags   the member's modifier flags
  entry #address   the address of the declaring tree in the class's scope, or `-1` for a Java member
  entry #erased   a Java member's erased signature, read off its descriptor; `Unset` for a Scala member

entry .Resolution   what a type reference turns out to name: a class, an alias, an abstract type, a term, a package
  detail
      `Universe#resolve` returns one. `Class(info, arguments, scope)` is a class with the type
      arguments it was applied to, if any. `Alias(arity, apply, scope, body, opaque)` is a
      type alias, opaque or not: `apply` substitutes arguments into its right-hand side,
      `body` is that right-hand side as declared (a lambda, when parameterized), and `opaque`
      says whether it is an opaque type, which erasure sees through and resolution does not.
      `Abstract(high, scope)` is an abstract type or type parameter, known only by its upper
      bound; `Term(tpe, scope)` a term, known by its declared type; `Package(path)` a
      package; and `Unknown(reason)` a reference the universe could not follow, with the
      reason in words. Each carries the scope its result is spelled in, for following it
      further.

entry .Universe   the classes a module's TASTy can reach, loaded lazily, and the semantic questions over them
  detail
      A `Universe` is built over a `Source` and loads from it on demand: a file's `Scope`
      the first time a path is asked for, a `ClassInfo` the first time a class is named, from
      the first of its TASTy or its classfile that exists. Over them it answers what the bytes
      of one file cannot: what a name refers to (`#resolve`), which class declares a selected
      member and which alternative an overloaded reference means (`#memberIn`, `#termsIn`,
      `#choose`), how a class linearizes (`#linearization`, `#derivesFrom`), and what type
      arguments a class hands its base (`#baseArguments`). Nothing here subtypes, infers or
      substitutes beyond an alias's parameters: erasure and member lookup need no more.

      It is exported to the umbrella as `Universe`, now that anthology's compiler universe is
      `Toolchain.Universe`, and holds the `Source` it reads from and the `ClassInfo` it builds. The companion holds the pure functions over
      the `Pickled` model which need no classpath: `typeOf`, `termType`, `methodType`,
      `substitute`, `transform`, `index`, `encode`, `classRef`, `className` and the rest.

      A class file's TASTy opened, its definitions listed, and one member's type erased:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Open a file and unpickle it
        code
            import soundness.*
            import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val file: Unpickler.File = Unpickler(cp"/scala/Option.tasty".read[Data])
            file.statements   // one `Tree.Package` for `scala`
      frame
        label Index it as a scope and list its definitions
        code
            import soundness.*
            import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val file: Unpickler.File = Unpickler(cp"/scala/Option.tasty".read[Data])
            val scope: Scope = degustation.Universe.index(t"scala/Option.tasty", file)
            scope.names.values.filter(_.s.startsWith("scala.Option."))
      frame
        label Build a universe over the classpath
        code
            import soundness.*
            import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val source: Universe.Source = new Universe.Source:
              def read(path: Text): Optional[Data] = threadContextClassloader.resource(path)
              def list(directory: Text): List[Text] = List()

            val universe = degustation.Universe(source)
            val option: Optional[Universe.ClassInfo] = universe.classOf(t"scala.Option")
            option.let(_.parents.map(degustation.Universe.className))
      frame
        label Find a member and erase its signature
        code
            import soundness.*
            import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val source: Universe.Source = new Universe.Source:
              def read(path: Text): Optional[Data] = threadContextClassloader.resource(path)
              def list(directory: Text): List[Text] = List()

            val universe = degustation.Universe(source)

            universe.classOf(t"scala.Option").let: info =>
              universe.memberIn(info, t"map", scala.collection.immutable.Set(Kind.Def)).let: (owner, member) =>
                universe.erasure.signature(owner, member)   // t"(1,scala.Function1)scala.Option"
      ##
      ```

      A universe caches everything it loads and is not thread-safe: build one per reading. A
      name no source has gives `Unset` from `#classOf` rather than an error; `Erasure` turns
      that into `Erasure.Unresolved` only where a signature cannot be spelled without it.
  refer degustation.Universe.Source
  refer degustation.Scope
  refer degustation.Universe.ClassInfo
  refer degustation.Resolution
  refer degustation.Erasure
  entry .marker   a `scala.annotation.internal` marker annotation, as a tree
      `Universe.marker(t"Repeated")` is the `New` of the named class in
      `scala.annotation.internal`, as the compiler attaches to a parameter's type for a
      modifier a method type cannot otherwise represent.
  entry .synthetic   the classes the compiler defines with no classfile or TASTy behind them
      `scala.Any` (with the members the compiler gives it), `scala.AnyKind`,
      `scala.Matchable`, `scala.Nothing`, `scala.Null`, `scala.Singleton`, the repeated-
      parameter marker `scala.<repeated>`, `scala.&` and `scala.|`, and the function classes
      the compiler makes up by arity — `FunctionN` beyond 22 and every `ContextFunctionN` —
      each as a `ClassInfo`; `Unset` for any other name. `Universe#classOf` consults this
      before the classpath.
  entry .impureFunction   `scala.ImpureFunctionN` and `scala.ImpureContextFunctionN` as the aliases they are
      The compiler's own aliases, in no file: `[-T1, …, -TN, +R] =>> FunctionN[T1, …, TN,
      R]^`, given as a `Resolution.Alias` whose body is the lambda; `Unset` for any other name.
  entry .anyMembers   the members the compiler gives `Any`, with their signatures
      `==`, `!=`, `equals`, `##`, `hashCode`, `toString`, `getClass`, `isInstanceOf`,
      `asInstanceOf` and their `$`-wrapped forms, each a `Member` whose `erased` signature is
      the one the compiler assigns (`getClass` and `isInstanceOf` take a type parameter).
  entry .objectMembers   the members the compiler gives `AnyRef` beyond `java.lang.Object`'s
      `eq`, `ne`, `synchronized` and `getClass`, added to `java.lang.Object` when it is loaded.
  entry .functionArity   the arity of a function class the compiler synthesizes, or `-1`
      `scala.FunctionN` for N beyond the implemented 22, and any `scala.ContextFunctionN`,
      `scala.ImpureFunctionN` or `scala.ImpureContextFunctionN`.
  entry .encode   a class name as it is spelled in a classfile or `.tasty` file name
      Operator characters become their `NameTransformer` names (`*:` is `$times$colon`) and a
      dot — which a top-level-definition carrier's name may contain — becomes `$u002E`.
  entry .decode   the inverse of `.encode`, for a class name read from a file name
  entry .qualify   a package member's full name: `pkg.name`, or `name` alone in the empty or root package
  entry .nth   the element of a list at an index, or `Unset` when the index is out of range
  entry .pin   types leaving the file they were read in, pinned to it as `Type.Foreign`
      Arguments substituted into another file's alias or value class keep resolving their
      local references in their own scope; with no scope to pin to, the types are returned as
      they are.
  entry .primitives   the nine primitive value classes, `scala.Unit` to `scala.Double`, by full name
  entry .classRef   a reference to a class by fully-qualified name, as a `Type`
      `Universe.classRef(t"scala.Option")` is `TypeRef(t"Option", TermRefPkg(t"scala"))`;
      a name with no package refers into `<empty>`. A Java nested class's binary `$` is read
      as a `.` in its name.
  entry .className   the fully-qualified name a class type names, when it can be read off the type alone
      Follows `TypeRefPkg`, `TypeRef` and `TypeRefIn` through package, `this` and stable-term
      prefixes, and `Applied` and `Annotated` to their underlying type; `Unset` for a type
      whose name needs resolution — a local reference, a type parameter, an alias.
  entry .upper   the upper bound a bounds type imposes, or the type itself when it is not bounds
  entry .typeOf   the type a type tree denotes
      `IdentTpt` and `Path` carry their type; `SelectTpt` becomes a `TypeRef` against its
      qualifier's `termType`; `SingletonTpt` its reference's type; `AppliedTpt` an `Applied`
      (or an `And`/`Or`, when the constructor is `scala.&` or `scala.|` written as a type
      tree); `RefinedTpt`, `LambdaTpt`, `BoundsTpt`, `AnnotatedTpt`, `MatchTpt` and
      `ByNameTpt` their corresponding types, with a lambda's parameters bound by address as a
      `Type.Abstraction`.
  entry .termType   the type of a term tree used as a type's prefix, or of a parent constructor call
      `Ident` carries its type; `Select` and `SelectIn` become term references against their
      qualifier; `QualifiedThis` a `This`; `New` its type; `Apply` the result of applying its
      function's type, or an `AppliedTerms` to be resolved later when that type is not yet at
      hand; a `Block` its expression's; and anything else `scala.Nothing`.
  entry .memberInfo   a type member's info as the compiler keeps it: an alias is bounds which coincide
  entry .annotationType   the class type an annotation tree constructs
      Looks through the `Apply`, `TypeApply`, `Select` of `<init>` and `New` of an annotation's
      constructor call to the annotated class, applied to any explicit type arguments.
  entry .parentType   the class type a parent clause names: the type under its constructor call, if any
  entry .methodType   the methodic type a definition's clauses and result spell
      `Universe.methodType(address, clauses, returns)` folds a `DefDef`'s clauses into nested
      `Type.Poly` and `Type.Method` types with the definition's own address as every clause's
      binder, so a `Type.Param` reference inside them resolves through
      `Universe#clauseAddresses`. This is how a `Member#tpe` for a method is built.
  entry .substitute   the parameters of the lambda at a binder replaced by arguments, throughout a type
  entry .replace   local references by address replaced by types, throughout a type
      Replaces `TypeRefDirect`, `TypeRefLocal`, `TermRefDirect` and `TermRefLocal` references
      to the given addresses: type parameters in a type, or term parameters whose singleton
      types a dependent method type mentions.
  entry .transform   rewrites every subtype for which a partial function is defined, bottom-up
  entry .mapChildren   rebuilds a type with a function applied to each of its immediate component types
  entry .index   indexes an unpickled file into a `Scope`
      Walks the file's trees, entering every definition by address with the fully-qualified
      name the compiler's `fullName` would give it: members of a class or package qualified by
      it, and a method's parameters and local types with the compiler's `_$` filler for each
      enclosing term (`pkg.Class._$param`). Definitions inside term bodies are recorded as
      local; a local type keeps its qualified name too, since an inline body refers to it by
      it. `Universe#scope` does this for a file found on the classpath.
  entry .children   the immediate subtrees of a tree
  entry #erasure   the `Erasure` over this universe, made once
  entry #scope   the scope of the `.tasty` file at a path, loaded and indexed once, or `Unset`
  entry #enter   a file supplied directly rather than found on the classpath, entered under a path
      The module being atomized, say: its classes become resolvable like any other's. Returns
      the `Scope` made for it.
  entry #classOf   the class named by a full name, or the class a type refers to
      `classOf(fullName)` gives the `ClassInfo` from the first of the class's synthetic
      definition, TASTy or classfile that exists, cached. A nested class lives in its
      top-level class's TASTy, and a class's simple name may itself contain dots (a
      top-level-definitions carrier is named after its source file), so every split of the
      name between package segments, a top-level class and nested classes is tried, longest
      package first; a module class `X$` is found in `X.tasty`. `classOf(tpe, scope)` is the
      class a type refers to, through aliases, bounds and singletons; both give `Unset` for
      what cannot be found.
  entry #resolve   what a type refers to, following local references in a scope
      The central question. Package references resolve to a class or a `Package`; named
      references to the member of their prefix, searched along its class's linearization and
      through an intersection's parts; local references to the definition at their address;
      an applied alias to whatever its expansion resolves to, with the arguments pinned to
      the scope they were spelled in; a `Param` to its binder's bound or parameter; a method
      type to its result; a literal to its constant's class. What cannot be followed is a
      `Resolution.Unknown` with the reason in words, never an exception.
  entry #parts   the two parts of a prefix that is an intersection or a union, or `Unset`
      Spelled as `And`/`Or`, or as an application of `scala.&` or `scala.|` (or an alias of
      one), whose members are those of its parts, the first part that has the member
      providing it.
  entry #clauseAddresses   the addresses of the parameters of a definition's clause, by the clause's names
      Which is how a `Type.Param(binder, number)` of a `methodType` is tied back to the
      parameter definition it stands for. Empty when the binder is not a definition's (a
      synthetic member's, say).
  entry #applicationType   the type of an application of a method reference, with the arguments substituted
      A type application of a polymorphic method, or a term application of a method, gives
      the method's result with the arguments in place of the parameters — by address for a
      definition's, by position for a synthetic member's — which is where a dependent result
      comes to refer to an argument. A reference that is no application resolves to its
      declared type. Returns the type with the scope it is spelled in.
  entry #expansionOf   the expansion of an alias reference or application, with the scope it is spelled in, or `Unset`
  entry #choose   among the members of a name along a class's linearization, the one a reference denotes
      The one whose signature the reference's `Name#signature` carries, if it is signed; an
      unsigned reference to an overloaded name denotes the parameterless alternative, since
      the pickler signs every other kind of method. A concrete member is preferred over an
      abstract redeclaration, as the compiler's `findMember` joins them.
  entry #baseArguments   the type arguments with which a class, applied to arguments, instantiates a base class
      The arguments themselves for the class itself; otherwise those of the parent clause
      which leads to the base, with the class's own parameters substituted; `Unset` when the
      class does not derive from the base.
  entry #packageObjectMember   a member of a package's package object or of one of its top-level-definition carriers
      Where a reference pickled against a package prefix finds a package-level type alias or
      definition: the `package$` object first, then each `<file>$package` carrier the
      `Source` lists in the package's directory.
  entry #owner   the class or package a prefix denotes: a package path, a class's `this`, a term's class
  entry #selfType   the self type a class declares, if any
  entry #memberIn   the member of a name and kinds, in a class or the first of its linearization that declares one
      Returns the member with its declaring class. The class's own declarations are searched
      first, then its linearization in order.
  entry #termsIn   every term member of a name along a class's linearization, declaring class first
  entry #linearization   the class's linearization by full name: itself, then its parents' from last to first
      `L(C) = C, L(Pn) +: … +: L(P1)`, keeping the last occurrence of a class that appears
      more than once, so a superclass's linearization is a suffix. A class asked for while its
      own linearization is being computed contributes only itself.
  entry #derivesFrom   whether a class has a base of the given full name in its linearization

  entry .Source   where a universe finds its classes: read an entry by path, list a directory
    detail
        A classpath reduced to two questions, so that the same semantic layer runs wherever the
        bytes can be fetched from: directories and jars on the JVM, a bundle in a browser, a
        linked-in archive in a native binary. Paths are slash-separated and relative, as
        classfiles and `.tasty` files are named: `scala/collection/immutable/List.tasty`.
        `#read` gives an entry's bytes or `Unset`; `#list` gives the simple names in a
        directory, which a `Universe` uses to find a package's top-level-definition carriers
        (the `<file>$package.tasty` files), and may be empty where listing is impossible, at the
        cost of package-level definitions resolving only through a package object.

        `Source(first, second, …)` combines sources, the first that has an entry answering
        for it. `Degustation.atomize` takes the dependency classpath as one, and `Inspection`
        builds one over directories, jars and the JDK's classes.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

        val source: Universe.Source = new Universe.Source:
          def read(path: Text): Optional[Data] = threadContextClassloader.resource(path)
          def list(directory: Text): List[Text] = List()

        source.read(t"scala/Option.tasty").present
        ```
    refer degustation.Universe
    refer degustation.Inspection
    entry .apply   combines several sources: the first that has an entry answers for it
        `read` returns the first source's answer that is present; `list` is the union of every
        source's listing, without duplicates.
    entry #read   the bytes of the entry at a slash-separated relative path, or `Unset`
    entry #list   the simple names of the entries directly in a directory (`t""` for the root)
  entry .ClassInfo   a class as the universe knows it, from its TASTy or its classfile
    detail
        `Universe#classOf(fullName)` gives one. It carries the class's `#fullName`, `#flags`,
        `#typeParams` (name and bounds) with the `#typeParamAddresses` the class's own types
        refer to them by, `#parents` as types, `#members`, the underlying type `#valueParam` of
        a class extending `AnyVal`, the `#scope` of the file it was read from (`Unset` for a
        Java class or a synthetic one), whether it is `#java`, and the `#address` of its
        definition in its scope (`-1` without TASTy).

        A Java class's members come from its classfile's descriptors, with the members the
        compiler gives `Any` and `AnyRef` added to `java.lang.Object` and the `+` the compiler
        gives `java.lang.String`; its interfaces are its parents, and a Java interface with no
        superclass has `scala.Any`, so that a universal trait of Java's is treated as the
        compiler treats it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

        val source: Universe.Source = new Universe.Source:
          def read(path: Text): Optional[Data] = threadContextClassloader.resource(path)
          def list(directory: Text): List[Text] = List()

        val universe = degustation.Universe(source)

        universe.classOf(t"scala.Option").let: info =>
          (info.isTrait, info.typeParams.map(_(0)), info.members.map(_.name))
        ```
    refer degustation.Universe
    refer degustation.Member
    entry #fullName   the class's fully-qualified name, a nested class's joined to its owner's with `.`
    entry #flags   the class's modifier flags: `Trait`, `Object`, `Abstract`, `Final`, `Sealed`, `Case`, …
    entry #typeParams   each type parameter's name and bounds, in order
    entry #typeParamAddresses   the addresses of the type parameters' definitions, which the class's own types refer to
    entry #parents   the parent types, as the template's parent clauses name them
    entry #members   the class's own declarations, as `Member`s; not its inherited ones
    entry #valueParam   the underlying type of a class extending `AnyVal`, or `Unset`
    entry #scope   the scope of the file the class was read from; `Unset` for a Java or synthetic class
    entry #java   whether the class was read from a classfile rather than TASTy
    entry #address   the address of the class's definition in its scope; `-1` for a class without TASTy
    entry #pkg   the package the class is in: the owner of its top-level class
    entry #isTrait   whether the `Trait` flag is set; a Java interface counts
    entry #isObject   whether the `Object` flag is set: a module class
    entry #isValueClass   whether the compiler treats the class as a value class
        A class extending `AnyVal` that is not one of the primitives (nor `AnyVal` or `Null`
        themselves), which is the rule the compiler applies, and which decides whether a
        parameter of that type erases to its underlying type.

entry .Erasure   Scala 3's type erasure over the `Pickled` model, as far as spelling an erased signature needs it
  detail
      An `Erasure` is made over a `Universe` — `universe.erasure` is the one made for it —
      and reproduces the rules of the compiler's `TypeErasure` and `Signature`, for Scala 3
      source, with value classes semi-erased, in signature-name mode: the setting under
      which the compiler spells the signature it stores in a `SIGNED` name. A member's
      signature is `(paramSigs)resultSig`, each part a fully-qualified erased class name or a
      type-parameter count: `(1,scala.Function1)scala.Option` for `Option#map`. Those are the
      disambiguators in `ScalaAtom` keys and in `Name#signature`, so the two sides of a
      reference agree by construction.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      val source: Universe.Source = new Universe.Source:
        def read(path: Text): Optional[Data] = threadContextClassloader.resource(path)
        def list(directory: Text): List[Text] = List()

      val universe = degustation.Universe(source)
      val erasure: Erasure = universe.erasure

      erasure.sigName(degustation.Universe.classRef(t"scala.Int"), Unset)      // t"scala.Int"
      erasure.sigName(degustation.Universe.classRef(t"scala.Any"), Unset)      // t"java.lang.Object"
      erasure.resultSigName(degustation.Universe.classRef(t"scala.Unit"), Unset)   // t"scala.Unit"
      ```

      The rules, in brief: `Any`, `AnyVal`, `Matchable`, `Singleton` and `caps.Pure` erase to
      `java.lang.Object`; `Tuple` and `NonEmptyTuple` to `scala.Product`; `Unit` to
      `scala.runtime.BoxedUnit` as a parameter and `scala.Unit` as a result; a value class to
      its underlying type as a parameter and to itself as an array element; an alias or
      opaque type to its right-hand side; an abstract type or type parameter to its upper
      bound; a by-name parameter to `scala.Function0`; a polymorphic function type to the
      `FunctionN` of its arity; a `*:` tuple to `TupleN` or `TupleXXL`; an `Array[T]` to an
      array of `T`'s erasure, or to `Object` when `T` is generic; an intersection to the
      erased greatest lower bound of its parts (dropping a pure capability part); a union to
      the erased least upper bound; a match type to its reduction, or its bound.

      A type whose erasure needs a class the universe cannot find throws
      `Erasure.Unresolved`, which `Inspection.atomize` reports as `Inspection.Error` with
      `Reason.Unencodable`.
  refer degustation.Universe
  refer degustation.Pickled
  entry .Erased   an erased type: a reference to a class by full name, or an array of one
      `Ref(name)` or `Array(element)`; `Erasure#render` spells them as `name` and `name[]`.
  entry .Unresolved   thrown when a type's erasure needs a class the universe cannot find
    detail
        A `RuntimeException` carrying `#detail`: the reason the universe gave, or the package
        which was used as a type. Erasure can fail only on a classpath that lacks something
        the module refers to, so the dependency classpath handed to `Degustation.atomize` or
        `Inspection.atomize` must carry everything the files reference, including the Scala
        library and the JDK's classes.
    entry #detail   why the type could not be resolved
  entry #signature   the erased signature of a definition, or of a member found by lookup
      `signature(tree, scope, owner)` is a `DefDef`'s signature: each type clause as its
      parameter count, each term parameter's erased name (an `erased` parameter, or one whose
      type is an `erased class`, has no place in it), and the result's; a constructor's
      result is its own class, erased as the class is. Anything that is not a method — a
      value, a parameterless method — has the signature the compiler gives a non-method, `()`.
      `signature(owner, member)` is a member's from lookup: a Java member's is read off its
      descriptor, a Scala member's from its declaration in its class's file.
  entry #signatureOf   the signature of a pickled methodic type — a refinement's method, say — spelled as a declaration's would be
  entry #javaSignature   a Java member's signature from its descriptor types, which are already erased
  entry #sigName   the erased name of a type as a parameter: `render(erase(tpe, scope, vc = true))`
  entry #resultSigName   the erased name of a type as a result: as a parameter's, except that `Unit` stays `scala.Unit`
  entry #render   an `Erased` spelled as a signature does: the class's full name, with `[]` for each array level
  entry #erase   the erasure |T| of a type, in a scope, with or without value classes semi-erased
      `vc` is the compiler's `semiEraseVCs`: `true` erases a value class to its underlying
      type, as a parameter is erased; `false` keeps it as itself, as an array element or the
      underlying of another value class is. Throws `Erasure.Unresolved` for a type the
      universe cannot follow.
  entry #erasedGlb   the erased greatest lower bound: one of its two arguments, by the compiler's total order
      Arrays before non-arrays, primitives before others, classes before traits, subclasses
      before superclasses, then by name. This is how an intersection erases.
  entry #erasedLub   the erased least upper bound, as a union erases
      `Nothing` vanishes against anything, `Null` against a type that derives from `Object`
      (a universal trait does not); arrays join elementwise; two classes join at the last
      minimal common base class, preferring classes over traits, or at `java.lang.Object`.

entry .Classfile   the little of a Java classfile that erasure and member lookup need
  detail
      A reader of the class's name, kind and supertypes, and its members' names and
      descriptors — which, for a Java member, already *are* its erased signature. Nothing
      generic is read: a Java type parameter erases to its bound, which the descriptor spells
      out, and the erasure of a Scala reference to a Java class is the class. A `Universe`
      reads one for every class it finds a `.class` file but no `.tasty` for, which is how
      the JDK's and any Java library's classes join the universe.

      It is `@unexported` from the umbrella, where `Classfile` is mandible's name, so it is
      written `degustation.Classfile`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val cls: degustation.Classfile.Class = degustation.Classfile(cp"/java/lang/String.class".read[Data])
      (cls.name, cls.superclass, cls.interfaces, cls.isInterface)
      cls.methods.filter(_.name == t"charAt").map(_.descriptor)   // List(t"(I)C")
      ```
  refer degustation.Universe
  entry .apply   reads a classfile's constant pool, header and member tables; attributes are skipped
      Of the class's own attributes only `InnerClasses` is read, for the entry describing the
      class itself: its enclosing class and whether it is static there. Bytes that do not
      begin with the classfile magic, or an unknown constant-pool tag, throw
      `Classfile.Malformed`.
  entry .Class   a class as read: name, flags, supertypes, members, and its enclosing class if nested
    detail
        `#name` is the dotted binary name; `#superclass` is `Unset` only for `java.lang.
        Object`; `#outer` and `#static` come from the `InnerClasses` attribute, and tell a
        `Universe` to name a nested class under its outer (`Outer.Inner`, or `Outer$.Inner`
        for a static one, which Scala sees as a member of the companion).
    entry #name   the class's name, with `.` between its package segments
    entry #flags   the class's access flags, as the classfile stores them
    entry #superclass   the superclass's name, or `Unset` for `java.lang.Object`
    entry #interfaces   the names of the interfaces the class implements
    entry #fields   the class's fields
    entry #methods   the class's methods, including constructors and static ones
    entry #outer   the binary name of the enclosing class, from `InnerClasses`, or `Unset`
    entry #static   whether a nested class is static within its outer
    entry #isInterface   whether the `ACC_INTERFACE` flag is set
  entry .Method   a method's name, descriptor and access flags
    entry #name   the method's name; `<init>` for a constructor
    entry #descriptor   the JVM descriptor, `(I)C` for `charAt`
    entry #flags   the method's access flags
  entry .Field   a field's name, descriptor and access flags
    entry #name   the field's name
    entry #descriptor   the JVM descriptor of the field's type
    entry #flags   the field's access flags
  entry .Descriptor   a descriptor's types as `Pickled.Type`s
    detail
        References to the named classes, with primitives as the Scala value classes they
        correspond to (`I` is `scala.Int`, `V` is `scala.Unit`) and arrays as applications of
        `scala.Array`.
    entry .parameters   the parameter types of a method descriptor
    entry .result   the result type of a method descriptor
    entry .field   the type of a field descriptor
  entry .Malformed   thrown for bytes that are not a classfile, or a descriptor that cannot be parsed
    entry #detail   what was wrong: _not a classfile_, _unknown constant pool tag N_, _bad descriptor …_

entry .Degustation   the atomizer: a module's TASTy files cut into the `ScalaAtom`s of LIRA's Scala discipline
  detail
      `Degustation.atomize` applies the atomization rules of the Scala discipline (`tasty/2`)
      over the `Pickled` model of a module's files, with a `Universe` answering the two
      questions the bytes cannot: what a reference names, and what a declaration's
      parameters erase to. The folding principle governs every decision: a declaration whose
      addition cannot break consumers — a class, a method, a value, a type member — stands
      alone as an atom, keyed by its fully-qualified name and erased signature; a fragment
      whose addition *can* break consumers — an abstract member of an open trait, a child of a
      sealed type, a parameter — folds into its enclosing atom's value, so that the whole
      compatibility check between two versions of a library stays set arithmetic over keys
      and values.

      Canonicalization is structural, never printed: symbols appear as fully-qualified names,
      types as a tag-length-value fold over the type constructors, type-lambda parameters as
      de Bruijn indices, and members in sorted key order. Positions, tool versions and
      sharing never enter the encoding, so recompiling a module, or reordering its
      declarations, yields byte-identical atoms; adding a concrete method leaves every other
      atom unchanged; and changing an inline body changes that body's atom alone. A construct
      outside the encoder's vocabulary is a hard error, `Degustation.Unencodable`, so
      vocabulary drift in the toolchain is detected rather than silently absorbed.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val dependencies: Universe.Source = new Universe.Source:
        def read(path: Text): Optional[Data] = threadContextClassloader.resource(path)
        def list(directory: Text): List[Text] = List()

      val files: List[(Text, Data)] =
        List((t"scala/Option.tasty", cp"/scala/Option.tasty".read[Data]))

      val atoms: List[ScalaAtom] = Degustation.atomize(files, dependencies)
      atoms.map(_.key).filter(_.s.startsWith("scala.Option.map("))
      ```

      Private members are not API and yield no atom; a `private[pkg]` member is
      conservatively API; an export forwarder atomizes exactly as its hand-written
      equivalent; an inline or macro definition yields a *replaceable* atom, keyed with an
      `[inline]` suffix, whose references name what its body splices. `Inspection.atomize`
      is this over files and a classpath on disk.
  refer degustation.ScalaAtom
  refer degustation.Inspection
  refer degustation.Universe
  entry .atomize   atomizes a module's `.tasty` files, each named by its classpath-relative path, against a dependency classpath
      The files are `(path, bytes)` pairs, the path relative to the classpath as a `.tasty`
      file is named (`fixture/Overloads.tasty`), and are entered into the universe before the
      dependencies, so the module's own classes resolve first. `dependencies` must carry
      everything the files reference, including the Scala library and the JDK's classes;
      a reference it cannot resolve throws `Erasure.Unresolved` where a signature needed it.
      References collected from inline bodies are classified at the end: those matching an
      atom of the module are `ScalaReference.Own`, the rest `ScalaReference.Foreign`, to be
      resolved against dependencies at assembly time.
  entry .Unencodable   thrown for a construct outside the encoder's vocabulary
    detail
        A `RuntimeException` carrying the `#construct` which could not be encoded. It means a
        form of TASTy the discipline has no canonical encoding for was met, and is the signal
        that the vocabulary needs extending; `Inspection.atomize` reports it as
        `Inspection.Error` with `Reason.Unencodable`, or `Reason.DuplicateKey` when the
        construct is a key produced twice.
    entry #construct   the construct, in words

entry .ScalaAtom   one atom of the Scala discipline, before hashing: key, class, canonical encoding and references
  detail
      The unit of LIRA's compatibility check. `#key` is the stable key: the definition's
      fully-qualified name with, for a method, its erased signature — `fixture.Overloads.
      f(scala.Int)scala.Int` — and an `[inline]` suffix for a replaceable atom.
      `#replaceable` is whether the atom is an inline or macro body, which a consumer copies
      and so depends on by value, rather than a rigid declaration it links against.
      `#encoding` is the canonical encoding whose hash is the atom's value, and
      `#references` the symbolic references collected from a replaceable body, empty for a
      rigid atom.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val atom = ScalaAtom(t"fixture.Tops.value", false, Data())
      atom.key
      atom.references
      ```
  refer degustation.Degustation
  refer degustation.ScalaReference
  entry #key   the stable key: fully-qualified name, erased signature for a method, `[inline]` for a replaceable atom
  entry #replaceable   whether the atom is an inline or macro body rather than a rigid declaration
  entry #encoding   the canonical encoding whose hash is the atom's value
  entry #references   the references a replaceable body makes; empty for a rigid atom

entry .ScalaReference   a symbolic reference from an inline body: to an atom of the same module, or of a dependency
    `Own(key)` names an atom of the same module by its key; `Foreign(key)` names one in a
    dependency, resolved to a value hash at assembly time, when the dependency's atoms are at
    hand. `Degustation.atomize` classifies every reference it collected by whether the key
    is among the module's own atoms.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val reference: ScalaReference = ScalaReference.Foreign(t"scala.Option.map(1,scala.Function1)scala.Option")

    reference match
      case ScalaReference.Own(key)     => t"own: $key"
      case ScalaReference.Foreign(key) => t"foreign: $key"
    ```

entry .Inspection   atomizes `.tasty` files on disk against a classpath of directories and jars
  detail
      The filesystem face of `Degustation.atomize`, in `degustation.core`. `Inspection.
      atomize(tastyFiles, classpath)` reads each file and each classpath directory through the
      galilei `FilesystemBackend on Linux` in scope, and each jar through zeppelin, builds a
      `Universe.Source` over them, and atomizes. No compiler is involved, so neither
      `scala3-compiler` nor `scala3-tasty-inspector` need be on the classpath to call it.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem

      val atoms: List[ScalaAtom] =
        Inspection.atomize(
          List(t"/build/classes/fixture/Overloads.tasty"),
          List(t"/build/classes", t"/lib/scala3-library.jar", t"/lib/scala-library.jar"))

      atoms.map(_.key)
      ```

      Each `Text` must parse as an absolute Linux path. Every classpath entry is a directory
      or a jar, by what is on disk; the classpath must carry everything the files reference,
      including the Scala library. The JDK's own classes are read from the running JVM's
      class loader on the JVM; on Scala Native there is no JVM to read them from, so a jar of
      the JDK's class files must be on the classpath. A file inside one of the classpath
      directories is named by its path relative to it; a file outside every one is placed by
      the package it declares, read from the file itself.

      A malformed path, an unreadable file or a malformed jar raises `Inspection.Error` with
      `Reason.InspectionFailed`; a construct the encoder cannot encode raises it with
      `Reason.Unencodable`, and a key produced twice with `Reason.DuplicateKey`. The atoms
      are those `Degustation.atomize` produces: byte-identical for a recompilation or a
      reordering of declarations, and the discipline they implement is `tasty/2`.
  refer degustation.Degustation
  refer degustation.ScalaAtom
  entry .atomize   atomizes the named `.tasty` files against the named classpath entries
      Needs a `FilesystemBackend on Linux` (`import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem` on
      the JVM, `filesystemBackends.scalaNativeFilesystem` on Native) and a
      `Tactic[Inspection.Error]`. Jars are read lazily: a jar's central directory is parsed
      once, and each entry's bytes when a class in it is first asked for.
  entry .Error   raised when the Scala discipline cannot be applied to the files
    detail
        Error 642: _the Scala discipline failed because `reason`_, where the reason is one of
        `Inspection.Error.Reason`'s cases: `InspectionFailed(detail)` (reason 1), _the TASTy
        could not be read: `detail`_, for a malformed path, an unreadable file, a malformed
        jar or a malformed classfile; `Unencodable(construct)` (reason 2), _no canonical
        encoding is defined for `construct`_, for a form the encoder has no vocabulary for or a
        type whose erasure needs a class the classpath lacks; and `DuplicateKey(key)` (reason
        3), _the key `key` was produced twice_. Raised by `Inspection.atomize` alone; the
        library's lower layers throw `Degustation.Unencodable`, `Erasure.Unresolved` and
        `Classfile.Malformed` instead, which `Inspection` translates.
    entry .Reason   why the discipline failed: `InspectionFailed`, `Unencodable` or `DuplicateKey`
        Each case carries its detail — the text of what could not be read, the construct, or
        the key — and the reason's number, 1 to 3, which becomes the error's sub-number.
    entry .communicable   renders each `Reason` as a message
        In implicit scope through the companion; nothing need be imported.
    entry #reason   the `Reason` the error carries
